{"availability":"available","full":"Castle639/domino-calibrator","name":"domino-calibrator","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2026-10-02T18:06:31Z","updated":"2026-10-03T12:41:09Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":null,"color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Version 0.1.0. MIT license (LICENSE); to cite it, CITATION.cff, or its DOI, (all its versions, on Zenodo). The files named here are in its repository, the installed package holds the code only.","A comet's photocentre is not its nucleus: an asymmetric coma pulls the centroid off, by an amount that depends on seeing, pixel scale, aperture and signal-to-noise. The shrinking-aperture method (D. J. Tholen; published form in Farnocchia et al. 2016, Icarus, arXiv:1507.01980, §2.1 and §3.1) measures the photocentre in synthetic apertures of radius 2.0–6.0 px in steps of 0.1 px, fits a straight line to x(r) and y(r), and takes its value at r = 0.","This package is an open, tested implementation of that method, with a command line and a plain page: the same method, ported and checked against the Python. The folder also holds the study that tested the method on Hubble's images of 3I/ATLAS: its scripts, logs and summaries are in runs/, and what it found is below, with how far each finding was checked and its limits, so that anyone can check it."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Castle639/domino-calibrator/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"91b253dffc725cace93fa7925bd38652a14bfe9bcb6609cda3d483025e97fb57","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T14:13:48.094Z"},"overviewCh